// ============================================================
// ============================================================
// Lone Wolf Trading Group - Multi-Instrument Trading Strategy (LWTG MITS)
// Replicates 9 confluences + RSI/ADX filters + dynamic exits
// Instruments: MES(5m), MNQ(15m), SIL(5m), MGC(5m), M2K(5m) + 10 Forex pairs (XAUUSD on 15m)
// Version: v3.1.8
// ============================================================
// Preset System:
//   Select instrument from dropdown — one preset per instrument
//   Qty set independently via Contracts input (works for any count)
//   When TP2 > 0 and qty >= 2: ceil(qty/2) exit at TP1 with BE stop on remainder to TP2
// ============================================================
// Exit Logic:
//   TP1/SL1 mode: full position exits at TP1, protected by SL1
//   TP1/TP2 split mode: ceil(qty/2) exits at TP1, stop moves to BE,
//   remainder exits at TP2. SL1 active until TP1 hit.
// ============================================================
// LWTG MITS (Lone Wolf Trading Group — Multi-Instrument Trading Strategy) is a TradingView Pine Script
// strategy loosely built on the available details of the Quantcrawler Bulletproof framework. It
// uses a 9-confluence scoring system to identify high-probability entries on five independently optimized
// futures instruments, and exits via fixed ATR-based targets.
// A core design principle of LWTG MITS is minimum drawdown. Every instrument in the portfolio was selected
// and optimized with drawdown as a primary constraint — not just profit factor. The strategy takes quick,
// disciplined exits at fixed ATR-based targets rather than chasing extended moves, which keeps individual
// trade risk small and portfolio-level drawdown consistently low. This approach makes LWTG MITS well-suited
// for prop firm trading environments where daily loss limits and maximum drawdown thresholds are strictly
// enforced. The backtest results across all five instruments reflect this priority — combined maximum drawdown
// across the full portfolio runs well under 10% at one contract per instrument, with individual instruments
// ranging from 0.70% to 3.45%. Full credit and IP for the genesis of this strategy belongs to Aaron at Quantcrawler.com.
// LWTG MITS was written independently as a personal backtest and automation project by a lifetime Quantcrawler member.
// If you are not already a member, the free trial is strongly recommended — the strategies, trading education, community,
// and team support are unmatched.
// ============================================================
// ============================================================
// ============================================================
// ============================================================
// RELEASE NOTES v3.1.8 — FIX: TradeLog confluence logging was reading
// a stale, off-by-one-bar value
// - Confirmed via real trade data: TradeLog showed confluence=5 and 8
//   on MGC/M2K/MES trades that could only have fired at the locked
//   threshold of 9 (bullSignal/bearSignal require >=9 to ever trigger
//   a trade -- that gate was always correct, never the issue).
// - Root cause: wh_entryConf (used only for the TradeLog/exit-side
//   payload) was captured inside the fill-detection block, which fires
//   on "strategy.position_size != 0 and strategy.position_size[1]==0"
//   -- i.e. one bar AFTER the signal bar, since strategy() executes
//   orders on bar close. By that point bullScore/bearScore had already
//   moved on to the new bar's value, so the logged number reflected
//   the WRONG bar's confluence, not the one that actually triggered
//   the trade.
// - NOT affected: AlertLog's "entry" payload (sheetsLongEntryMsg/
//   sheetsShortEntryMsg) already correctly fires on the bullSignal/
//   bearSignal bar itself, same bar as the real score -- verified
//   correct, no fix needed there.
// - Fix: added pendingSignalConf, a persistent var captured at the
//   exact moment bullSignal/bearSignal evaluates true (the real signal
//   bar). wh_entryConf now reads this stored value instead of
//   re-deriving bullScore/bearScore one bar late at fill time.
// - Per explicit instruction: all logging must accurately reflect the
//   actual state of the parameters the code used at decision time, not
//   an approximation. This fix exists specifically to meet that bar.
// - v4.x was never affected -- its wh_entryConf-equivalent captures
//   based on ghostDir (an explicit signal-direction flag set at the
//   correct moment), not strategy.position_size timing.
// ============================================================
// RELEASE NOTES v3.1.7 — CRITICAL FIX: MES PnL was 10x overstated
// - Root cause: tl_tick lookup chain had NO explicit MES check. Since
//   "MES" contains the substring "ES", every MES trade fell through to
//   the generic ES (full-size E-mini) check and got tl_tick=50.0
//   instead of MES's real 5.0 -- a confirmed exact 10x PnL overstatement.
// - See v4.1.6 release notes for full detail (identical bug, same fix).
// - Every MES PnL $ figure logged before this fix should be treated as
//   unreliable (overstated 10x) until manually corrected.
// ============================================================
// RELEASE NOTES v3.1.6 — MGC re-optimized (original params traced to
// an untrustworthy 26-trade external-tool sweep)
// - Live trading (TradingView Strategy Tester export, 56 trades,
//   2026-05-04 to 2026-06-24) showed MGC at WR 73.2%, PF 0.786,
//   net -$1,632 -- LOSING money, well below the 76.9% breakeven WR
//   this instrument's 2.5:0.75 SL:TP ratio requires.
// - Investigated: original MGC params (conf=9, wt_chan=23, wt_avg=4,
//   swing_len=14) trace back to a QC Optimizer external-tool sweep on
//   only 26 trades. A prior session had already flagged that the
//   Python backtest engine "can't reproduce" MGC's claimed PF 10.118 --
//   that warning was never resolved or revisited until now.
// - Fresh walk-forward sequential sweep (conf -> C9 swing -> C8 vfi ->
//   C7 atr_mult -> C3 wt_chan/wt_avg -> C4 mom_len) on 3.4yr data:
//   conf=9 CONFIRMED correct (clean monotonic peak, OOS PF 3.435 @ 101
//   trades -- holds up, this one wasn't the problem). wt_chan/wt_avg
//   WRONG -- 23/4 wasn't even close to optimal on proper re-test.
// - Changed: mgc_swingLen 14->16, mgc_wtChanLen 23->3, mgc_wtAvgLen
//   4->10, mgc_momLen 18->22. vfi_len, atr_mult confirmed near-optimal/
//   flat, unchanged. conf unchanged (already correct).
// - Best validated result: OOS PF ~4.7-5.5 depending on exact combo,
//   WR ~93%, ~108 trades. Real and well-sampled, but MUCH lower than
//   the original claimed 10.118/96.15% (that number was likely overfit
//   to a thin, favorable sample from the start).
// - IMPORTANT, UNRESOLVED: even this corrected backtest (OOS PF ~4.7-5.5)
//   does not explain the live PF 0.786 result. Fixing these four params
//   is a real improvement and should help, but there is currently no
//   evidence it fully closes the live-vs-backtest gap. Treat MGC as
//   "improved, not confirmed fixed" until more live data comes in.
//   Possible causes not yet investigated: regime shift in the live
//   window, slippage/commission modeling differences, Ghost execution
//   vs backtest fill assumptions.
// - corrected_presets.py (Python engine) updated to match.
// - FOLLOW-UP 2026-06-24: data_mgc_5m.txt used for the sweep above was
//   discovered to be built on a stale contract-roll assumption (went
//   dead after ~5/27, the real MGCM6->MGCQ6 roll, not 6/13 as the
//   original fetch script assumed). Rebuilt via Databento bulk "parent"
//   symbology with volume-based front-month detection across all 5
//   years -- clean, regular bi-monthly rolls confirmed, no anomalies.
//   Re-ran the full conf/C9/C8/C7/C3/C4 sequence on this corrected
//   data: EVERY param above held up, conf=9 and wt_chan=3 even
//   stronger (OOS PF 5.687 @ 169tr and 6.673 @ 159tr respectively).
//   No param changes from this re-check -- this note exists purely to
//   document that the values above are now verified on trustworthy
//   data, not just the thin/mis-rolled set the original sweep used.
// ============================================================
// RELEASE NOTES v3.1.5 — M2K re-optimized (walk-forward sequential sweep)
// - Fresh sequential sweep on 3.4yr M2K 3m data (conf -> rsi/sl/tp ->
//   C9 swing -> C8 vfi -> C7 atr_mult -> C3 wt_chan/wt_avg -> C4 mom_len),
//   same methodology as MGC/SIL optimization. Single walk-forward split:
//   IS = last 12mo, OOS = prior 6mo (same standard as MES/MGC/SIL).
// - Result: OOS PF 1.843, WR 79.1%, 187 trades. Prior deployed config
//   (conf=8, this file) had never been walk-forward verified at this
//   exact value; m2k_conf=9 traces to a real 2026-04-01/v3.0.4 optimizer
//   run (OOS PF 1.171) but that result was thin. This sweep supersedes it.
// - Changed: m2k_conf 8->9, m2k_rsiOS 39->42, m2k_rsiOB 60->65 (had been
//   33 in earlier v3.0.4 state on this line in some prior versions —
//   see inline comments below for exact prior value), m2k_sl1 2.50->3.00,
//   m2k_tp1 2.00->1.00, m2k_swingLen 7->8, m2k_wtChanLen 20->6,
//   m2k_wtAvgLen 4->3.
// - vfi_len, atr_mult, mom_len tested and confirmed flat/near-optimal —
//   left unchanged.
// - Corrected preset header comment: M2K runs on 3m, not 5m (typo in
//   prior versions' block comment carried forward since v3.0.3).
// - corrected_presets.py (Python engine) updated to match.
// ============================================================
// RELEASE NOTES v3.1.4 — script_version field in GAS payloads
// - Added "script_version" as first field in sheetsLongEntryMsg,
//   sheetsShortEntryMsg, and exitLogMsg. Hardcoded to "v3.1.3" (this
//   file's identity at time of edit — bump manually each version).
// - Lets AlertLog/TradeLog rows be filtered/identified by which Pine
//   file (v3.x vs v4.x, and which exact version) produced them, since
